## 1 Introduction
2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (picric acid, PA), a well-known explosive with high explosive power, is also extensively used in dyes, pharmaceutical, fireworks, glass and leather industries [1,2]. Besides, picric acid is also recognized as a highly toxic pollutant. When PA is released into the environment, it will cause serious pollution to the soil and water, and then harm human health [3–6]. Furthermore, because of the electron-deficient character of PA, natural degradation of PA is very difficult [7]. Thus, it is of considerable importance to detect PA with high selectivity and sensitivity for homeland security and environmental protection [8]. Currently, there are several methods for the detection of nitro explosives including gas chromatography coupled mass spectrometry, surface enhanced Raman spectroscopy, cyclic voltammetry and ion mobility spectroscopy (IMS) [9–13]. Compared to those detection methods, direct fluorescence-based detection methods have exhibited significant advantages such as high selectivity and sensitivity, low detection limit, good portability, short response time and low cost [1,4].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [14] have been extensively used in supercapacitors [15], gas adsorption and separation [16,17], catalysis [18,19] and chemical sensing, of which luminescent metal-organic frameworks (LMOFs) have emerged as important sensing materials to detect small molecules [20,21], metal ions [22–26], anions [27,28], explosives [1–7,29–31], pH [32–34], temperature [35,36] and so on. More recently, some LMOFs displaying high selectivity and sensitivity for the detection of PA have been reported [5,37–39]. To obtain LMOFs with high selectivity and sensitivity for sensing of PA, it is very important to choose electron-rich ligands [1,40–42]. 9H-Carbazole-2,7- dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{L})$ , containing potential hydrogen bond donor N–H, is a kind of electron-rich ligand and an excellent luminescent material. At present, several transition metalbased MOFs [43–45] have been synthesized based on this ligand. In 2017, Biswas et al. [45] reported a $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF based on $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ that can act as a multi-responsive sensor to selectively detect $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{CN}^{-}$ ions as well as $p$ -nitrophenol (PNP). Compared to luminescent transition metal-organic frameworks, lanthanide-based MOFs (LnMOFs) have attracted considerable attentions owing to the excellent optical properties such as large Stokes shifts and high color purity. However, to the best of our knowledge, there is no report about LnMOFs involving $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ ligand.
Based on the above considerations, we synthesized a 3D LnMOF $\mathrm{\small{[Eu_{3}L_{3}(C H_{3}C O O)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}(\upmu_{3}-O H)]bullet3D M F}}$ (EuL) by solvothermal synthesis based on the ligand $9H.$ -carbazole2,7-dicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}\right)$ . The investigations on the luminescence property and luminescence response for the nitro compounds reveal that EuL is a potential luminescent sensor material for PA with high sensitivity and selectivity. The possible sensing mechanism towards PA is also discussed.
