## 2.2 Synthesis of $\mathrm{\small{[Eu_{3}L_{3}(C H_{3}C O O)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}(\upmu_{3}-O H)]}}$ •3DMF
A mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ ( $10.2\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.04\mathrm{mmol}$ ), $\mathrm{Eu(NO_{3})_{3}{\bullet}6H_{2}O}$ ( $17.88\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.04\mathrm{mmol}$ ), DMF $(5\mathrm{mL})$ ), water $(0.3~\mathrm{mL})$ and glacial acetic acid $(25\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was sealed in a $25~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflonlined stainless steel autoclave and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2{\mathrm{~d~}}$ The reaction mixture was slowly cooled to room temperature. Yellow block crystals of $\mathbf{EuL}$ were collected by filtration and washed with DMF several times. Elemental analysis calcd. for $\mathrm{C}_{55}\mathrm{H}_{53}\mathrm{Eu}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{22}$ $(\%)$ : C, 41.14; N, 5.23; H, 3.33. Found: C, 40.90; N, 5.39; H, 3.21. IR data (KBr, $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ): 3230 b, 2930 w, 1662 s, 1539 s, 1400 s, 1229 w, 775 s, 671 s.
EuL-DMF suspension were prepared by introducing $3\mathrm{mg}$ of EuL powder into $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and then ultrasonic agitation for 30 min before testing.
## 2.3 Crystal structure determination
X-ray diffraction data of the single crystals of EuL were collected on a Bruker Smart Apex CCD area detector diffractometer using graphitemonochromated Mo-Kα radiation $(\lambda{=}0.71073\mathrm{~\AA})$ at $100\mathrm{K}$ . Cell parameters were retrieved using SMART software and refined using SAINT [46] on all observed reflections. Data were collected using a narrowframe method with scan widths of $0.30^{\circ}$ in $\omega$ . The highly redundant data sets were reduced using SAINT and corrected for Lorentz and polarization effects. Absorption corrections were applied using SADABS [47] supplied by Bruker. Structures were solved by direct methods using the SHELX97 [48] program package. The positions of the metal atoms and their first coordination spheres were located from directmethod E maps; other non-hydrogen atoms were found using alternating difference Fourier synthesis and least-squared refinement cycles and, during the final cycles, were refined anisotropically. The hydrogen atoms were placed in calculated positions and refined as riding atoms with a uniform value of $U_{\mathrm{iso}}$ . Isolated solvents within the channels were not crystallographically well-defined. The SQUEEZE routine in the PLATON software package [49] was applied to subtract the solvent contribution. According to the synthesis, elemental analytic data, $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectrum and TGA analyses, the compound is formulated as $\mathrm{\small{[Eu_{3}L_{3}-(C H_{3}C O O)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}}}$ $\left(\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}\right).$ ]•3DMF. Crystallographic details for EuL have been listed in Table S1 (Supporting Information online). The selected bond lengths and bond angles are showed in Table S2. The CCDC reference number for EuL is 1850425.
## 3 Results and discussion
EuL were obtained in the mixed solvents of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.3~\mathrm{mL})$ and DMF $(5~\mathrm{mL})$ ) under solvothermal conditions. The guest molecules in the channels of $\mathbf{EuL}$ are highly disordered. The type and quantity of the disordered guest molecules were determined by the $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ spectrum (Figure S1, Supporting Information online), TGA analysis (Figure S2) and elemental analysis. We recorded $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ spectrum of EuL digested by concentrated hydrochloric acid, in which the peaks of DMF with chemical shift 7.95, 2.90 and 2.74 ppm were observed. According to the integral intensities ratio of the peaks of $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ (3.08) and DMF (3.77), we inferred that the proportion of $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligand and guest DMF molecule is 1:1. It was further affirmed by the TGA curves. For the desolvated EuL obtained by heating sample at $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a vacuum drying oven for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ , there is little weight loss in the temperature range from the room temperature to $335^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . However, at the same temperature range, for untreated EuL sample, the weight loss is $16.1\%$ , which is very close to the calculated weight loss value of $15.9\%$ for three DMF molecules and two coordinated water molecules.
The infrared spectra of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ and EuL are shown in the Figure S3. The band at $1674~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in IR spectrum of free $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ is assigned to characteristic stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ of carboxyl groups. In the IR spectrum of EuL, two new bands at 1539 and $1400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ appear, assigned to vibrations of carboxylate groups, and the strong absorption band at $1662~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be attributed to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ vibration of DMF molecules. The presence of $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ are confirmed by the characteristic bands of $\mathrm{C-H}$ $2900{-}2975\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ vibrations. The broad band at $3242~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ should be attributed to stretching vibrations of the coordinated water molecules.
## 3.1 Crystal structure of EuL
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction reveals that EuL crystallizes in the triclinic space group $P\bar{1}$ (Table S1). The asymmetric unit of EuL contains three crystallographically independent ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions, three $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligands, a $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ion, two coordinated acetate ions, two coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules and three highly disordered DMF guest molecules (Figure 1(a)). All $\mathrm{Eu}^{\bar{3}+}$ ions are eight coordinated by oxygen atoms to form the distorted bicapped trigonal prismatic coordination geometry. Eu1 is coordinated by five oxygen atoms from five $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ , two oxygen atoms from two acetates and one oxygen atom from a $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ molecule. $\mathtt{E u}2$ is coordinated by four oxygen atoms from four $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ , three oxygen atoms from two acetates and one oxygen atom from a $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ion. Eu3 is coordinated by four oxygen atoms from three $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ , two oxygen atoms from two $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions, one oxygen atom from one acetate and one oxygen atom from a $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule. Each $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligand coordinates to four ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions by its four carboxylate oxygen atoms. The acetate in structure exhibits two coordination modes, that is two-connected $\mu_{2}$ mode and three-connected $\mu_{3}$ mode. Two $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ , two $\mu_{2}{\bf-O}12$ , two $\mu_{2}{\bf-O}13$ and two $\mu_{2}$ - O15 connect six ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions to form the $\mathrm{Eu}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ cluster. Each $\mathrm{Eu}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ cluster is linked to the other two $\mathrm{Eu}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ clusters by the carboxylate groups of ${\mathrm{L}}^{2-}$ ligands to form a series of parallelaligned Eu–O–C chains extending along the $c$ axis (Figure 1 (b)). The $\mathrm{Eu-O-C}$ chains are further linked with each other by the carbazole moieties of $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligands in the [010] and [110] directions to form the three-dimensional framework with one-dimensional channels of rhombic aperture with the diagonal lengths of $15{\times}27\mathrm{\AA}^{2}$ , in which the highly disordered DMF guest molecules are trapped (Figure 1(c)). The solvent accessible space for desolvated $\mathbf{EuL}$ is $1776\mathrm{\AA}^{3}$ per unit cell or $49\%$ of the total volume $(3646\mathrm{\AA}^{3})$ , calculated by PLATON program [49].
## 3.2 PXRD and TGA
The PXRD patterns of EuL are shown in Figure S4. Compared to the diffraction peaks of crystal planes (100) and (-110) with the $2\theta$ of $6.48^{\circ}$ and $6.90^{\circ}$ in the simulated PXRD pattern, both the diffraction peaks downshift to the smaller angles with the $2\theta$ of $6.06^{\circ}$ and $6.51^{\circ}$ in the experimental PXRD pattern. The simulated PXRD pattern was calculated from the single-crystal data collected at the temperature of $100\mathrm{K}$ . However, the experimental PXRD pattern was collected at the room temperature. The shifts of the diffraction peaks maybe due to the swelling of framework with the increase of temperature from the $100\mathrm{K}$ to room temperature. After being placed in air for more than $80\mathrm{min}$ or being dipped into water for $2\mathrm{h}$ , the two diffraction peaks of the assynthesized EuL powder sample upshift to larger angles with the $2\theta$ of $7.16^{\circ}$ and $7.55^{\circ}$ , suggesting the shrinking of framework, and the process is reversible upon dipping the powder sample into the solvent DMF. We speculate that the shrinking of framework is due to the entering of water molecules into channels and the formation of hydrogen bond interactions between the entering water molecules and the walls of channels. Because the cracks appear quickly in the crystal when the EuL samples were put in air and in water, it is impossible for us to collect the single crystal data to analyze the reason of the shrinking of framework from the viewpoint of structure. When the EuL samples are soaked in the water for more than $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the framework completely collapsed. However, in the other tested solvents, the integrity of framework is maintained after the EuL samples soaked for more than $24\mathrm{h}$ .
Since EuL has the rhombic channels and large pore volume, it prompts us to obtain the desolvated EuL to study its adsorption properties. We tried to remove the guest molecules in the channels of EuL by heating sample at $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a vacuum drying oven for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ . According to the TGA curves and PXRD patterns as showed in Figures S2 and S4, the framework still remained intact and the guest molecules in the channels had been completely removed. However, the $\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ absorption experiment display that the BET surface area of desolvated EuL is small (Figure S5), which maybe due to the partial collapse of the framework of the desolvated EuL.
The TGA curve of EuL was recorded in Figure S2. It reveals that in the temperature range from the room temperature to $335^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the weight loss of EuL is $16.1\%$ , corresponding to the release of three disordered DMF molecules (calc. $13.7\%$ ) in channel and two coordinated water molecules (calc. $2.2\%$ ). The weight loss of $8.6\%$ in the temperature range of $335{-}540^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is attributed to the departure of two coordinated acetate ions and a $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ion (calc. $8.5\%$ .
## 3.3 Photoluminescence properties
The excitation and fluorescence emission spectra of EuL in the solid state at room temperature are displayed in Figure S6. Upon excitation at $400\mathrm{nm}$ , the solid-state emission spectra of EuL reveal the strong characteristic emission peaks of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion at 580, 592, 615, 651 and $700\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the $\mathrm{{}^{5}D_{0}{\rightarrow}^{7}F_{0.4}}$ transitions. The emission originated from the $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligand almost completely disappear [43,45]. In complex, no ligand-based emission and strong metal-based emission suggest that the excitation energy is effectively transferred from the ligands to the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions.
## 3.4 Detection for nitro explosives
Based on our continuing research interest in fluorescent sensing for nitro explosives, we studied the fluorescent response of EuL for a series of nitro explosives such as PA, 2,4-dinitrotoluene (2,4-DNT), 3,4-dinitrotoluene (3,4-DNT), 4-nitrotoluene (4-NT), 2-nitrotoluene (2-NT), 1,3-dinitrobenzene (1,3-DNB), 1,2-dinitrobenzene (1,2-DNB), 2,3- dimethyl-2,3-dinitrobutane (DMNB) and nitrobenzene (NB). We collected the emission spectra of EuL-DMF suspension before and after addition of the different nitro explosives with the final nitro compound concentration of $0.1\mathrm{mM}$ upon excitation at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2). Interestingly, as presented in Figure 2, PA decreased by $83.4\%$ of luminescence intensity of EuL-DMF suspension when monitoring emission at $615\mathrm{nm}$ . However, compared to PA, other eight nitro explosives exhibited only negligible quenching percentages $\scriptstyle\mathrm{(QP=}(I_{0}{-}I)/I_{0}{\times}100^{0}\not\%$ , where $I_{0}$ and $I$ are fluorescence intensities of EuL-DMF suspension before and after addition of the nitro explosives) on the luminescence intensities of EuL-DMF suspension. These data unambiguously indicate that $\mathbf{EuL}$ possesses the remarkably high selectivity towards PA among the nitro explosives.
To better understanding the detection sensitivity of EuLDMF suspension for PA, the fluorescence titration experiments for PA were further carried out (Figure 3(a)). The results show that the luminescent intensities of EuL-DMF suspension drastically decreased with the addition of PA (from 0 to $0.15\mathrm{mM},$ ). Furthermore, the concentration-dependent luminescence data can be analyzed via the SternVolmer (S-V) equation, $I_{0}/I{=}K_{\mathrm{sv}}[\mathrm{Q}]{+}1$ $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ is the quenching constant, and [Q] is the concentration of the analyte). Upon excitation at $400\mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in Figure 3(b), the concentration of PA has a nonlinear relationship with $I_{0}/I$ in the whole tested concentration range of $0{-}0.15\ \mathrm{mM}$ . However, in the low concentration range of $0{-}0.025\ \mathrm{mM}$ , the good linear relationship with $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ of $29022\boldsymbol{\mathrm{M}}^{-1}$ $R^{2}{=}0.9935)$ , and in the high concentration range of $0.05{-}0.15~\mathrm{mM}$ , the good linear relationship with $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ of $98074{\mathrm{M}}^{-1}$ $R^{2}{=}0.9555)$ were observed, respectively. As far as we know, this $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ of 98074 $\mathbf{M}^{-1}$ is among the highest values for COFs and MOFs in detection of PA (Table S3) [50]. The detection limit of EuLDMF suspension towards PA is calculated as $6.58\times10^{-7}\mathrm{M}$ according to the titration data in the low concentration range, indicating that $\mathbf{EuL}$ is an excellent candidate of the sensing materials for PA.
Inspired by the high luminescence quenching efficiency of PA towards EuL, we conducted competitive fluorescence titration experiments to study the anti-interference ability of EuL for the detection of PA $(0.1\mathrm{mM})$ . The PA was gradually added to the EuL-DMF suspension containing the interfering nitro explosives (Figures S7–S14). As showed in Figure 4, the existence of other eight nitro explosives does not significantly interfere with the detection of PA. Therefore, EuL possesses good anti-interference ability for the detection of PA, even when other interfering nitro explosives coexist in the solution.
As a sensor material used in practical applications, the repeatability of the sensors is also essential. The five-cycle recovery experiments were performed to study the repeatability of EuL towards the detection of PA. After each fluorescence titration test, EuL was recovered by centrifugation and then washed with DMF several times. As illustrated in Figure 5, it is noted that EuL shows the similar fluorescence intensity and quenching percentages after each cycle, and the PXRD of EuL repeated after 5 times is matched with that of the as-synthesized samples (Figure S15). The results reveal that EuL retain high sensitivity towards the PA after repeated cycles.
## 3.5 The possible mechanism of fluorescence quenching
To better understand the high selectivity and sensitivity of EuL towards PA, we try to discuss the possible quenching mechanism. First of all, the fluorescence lifetimes of EuLDMF suspension with and without PA were measured to distinguish the interactions between PA and framework contributing to the fluorescence quenching from various possible interactions (Figure S16). As illustrated in Table S4, the fluorescence lifetimes of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion at $615\mathrm{nm}$ are $574{\pm}5~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ and almost consistent with the increase of the concentration of the PA from 0 to $0.1\mathrm{mM}$ , demonstrating that the fluorescence quenching of PA for EuL does not result from the interactions between PA and the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion in EuL. So the interactions between PA and carbazole dicarboxylate maybe lead to the fluorescence quenching of EuL.
According to previous reports [5,38,45], the fluorescent quenching response of MOFs and coordination polymers towards PA may be mainly attributed to the following three factors: (1) competitive absorption; (2) fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET); and/or (3) electrostatic interactions. So we discuss and investigate the contributions of the three factors to the fluorescence quenching of EuL one by one. Firstly, we collected the UV-Vis spectra of nine nitro explosives and free ligand in DMF (Figure S17). The result shows that PA has a wide absorption band centered at $370\mathrm{nm}$ and exhibits much stronger absorption ability than other eight nitro compounds in the wavelength range of $325{\mathrm{-}}475{\mathrm{~nm}}$ , which indicate that the competitive absorption for the excitation light $(400\mathrm{nm})$ is an important reason for the fluorescence quenching of EuL by PA. We wondered whether the detecting sensitivity could be further improved if using the maximum absorption wavelength of $370\mathrm{nm}$ of PA as the excitation light to excite the EuL. Then, the fluorescence titration experiments for PA were conducted upon excitation at $370\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S18). However, the result is different from our prediction. Upon excitation at $370\mathrm{nm}$ , the
$K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ values are $27335\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ in the low concentration range of $0{-}0.025\ \mathrm{mM}$ and $76072\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ in the high concentration range of $0.05{-}0.15\ \mathrm{mM}$ , respectively. Both values are smaller than 29022 and $98074\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ , respectively, obtained when the excitation wavelength is $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Therefore, we speculated that competitive absorption is an important reason for fluorescence quenching, but not the only reason. With indeed, as shown in Figure S19, we found that the absorption spectrum of PA significantly overlap with the emission spectrum of the ligand, illustrating that fluorescence resonance energy transfer from $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ to PA may occur in this detection system. Besides the competitive absorption and FRET mechanism, the electrostatic interactions between the sensing materials and analyte also have effect on the luminescence sensing response. According to the previous literature, the strong electrostatic interactions between the $-\mathrm{NH}$ group of the carbazole-based $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ fluorophore and the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group of $p$ - nitrophenol (PNP) may contribute to fluorescence quenching of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF [45]. Base on the above results, we believe that the highly selectivity and sensitivity of EuL for PA sensing should be attributed to the synergistic effect of competitive absorption, fluorescence resonance energy transfer as well as strong electrostatic interactions.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we succeeded in construction of a fluorescent MOF material EuL based on $9H\cdot$ -carbazole-2,7-dicarboxylic acid ligand. In crystal structure of $\mathbf{EuL}$ , the $\mathrm{Eu-O-C}$ chains are linked by the carbazole moieties of $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ ligands to form the three-dimensional framework with rhombic channels. EuL displays highly selectivity and sensitivity, good antiinterference ability and repeatability for PA sensing with the $K_{\mathrm{sv}}$ of $98074{\mathrm{M}}^{-1}$ in the PA concentration range of 0.05 to $0.15\mathrm{mM}$ , making EuL a potential luminescent sensory material for PA. According to the analysis and discussion on the mechanism of fluorescence quenching, we conclude that the synergistic effect of competitive absorption, fluorescence resonance energy transfer as well as strong electrostatic interactions make EuL display the highly selectivity and sensitivity for PA sensing.
